<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sudan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sudan refers both to the modern country on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and, historically, to the bilad al-Sudan, the broad belt of West and Central African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. Arab geographers used the term for the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu</a> empires. The term appears in many profiles, including those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sudan.jpg" alt="Sudan" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sudan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ezana's inscriptions, including the trilingual Ezana Stone in Ge'ez, Sabaean and Greek, record campaigns against peoples across what is now <b>Sudan</b>, Eritrea and the Ethiopian interior, presenting the king as a divinely sanctioned conqueror who destroyed towns and deported populations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 14th and 15th centuries Arab tribes overran much of <b>Sudan</b>, perhaps aided by a plague in the mid-14th century; the collapse of Makuria late in the century, in Adams's phrase, burst the flood gates open.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Al-Zubayr's defeat and killing of Sultan Ibrahim in 1874 ended the first sultanate, and Darfur was annexed to Egyptian <b>Sudan</b>; in 1883–1884 it fell to the forces of the Mahdi, and Keira authority remained in abeyance until 1898.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Kutama Berbers formed the original revolutionary core; Turkish slave soldiers, the ghilman, were recruited for their skill as mounted archers; Black African infantry from the <b>Sudan</b> formed a large and politically assertive contingent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A decree of 16 June 1895 placed Senegal, French <b>Sudan</b>, French Guinea and Ivory Coast under a governor-general, who was also governor of Senegal at Saint-Louis, to coordinate military and economic policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Many families of holy men whose authority dates from this period remained socially and politically important in the <b>Sudan</b> into the twentieth century, and the Qadiriyya and other orders introduced under the Funj became central to Sudanese religious life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
